{"claims":[{"excerpt":"## Who Counts as Unemployed?","claim":"Whether someone counts as unemployed depends on classification criteria.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Under the Current Population Survey, an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"Explain who is counted as unemployed—and therefore included in the unemployment-rate denominator—through two contrasting situations: a temporarily laid-off person expecting recall and someone who only browsed job listings.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The title frames a question answered by the source’s classification criteria.","materiality":"Frames the piece’s subject."},{"excerpt":"The unemployment rate is calculated using the number of unemployed people and the number of employed people—not everyone who does not have a job.","claim":"The unemployment rate uses the numbers of unemployed and employed people, rather than everyone without a job.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"Establish that the unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the combined number of employed and unemployed people, rather than by everyone without a job.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source specifies the employed-plus-unemployed denominator, which excludes people without jobs who are not classified as unemployed.","materiality":"Defines the rate’s population denominator."},{"excerpt":"To be counted as unemployed, a person generally must not have been employed during the reference week, have been available for work (with an exception for temporary illness), and have actively looked for work during the four-week period ending with that week.","claim":"Generally, unemployment classification requires no employment during the reference week, availability for work except in the case of temporary illness, and active job search in the four-week period ending with the reference week.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Under the Current Population Survey, an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"Preserve the conditions: the person must not have been employed during the reference week and must have been available for work, except for temporary illness. Active search must fall within the four-week period ending with the reference week, unless the person is temporarily laid off and expects recall.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The piece accurately states the general conditions; the temporary-layoff exception is separately described.","materiality":"These criteria determine who is classified as unemployed."},{"excerpt":"There is an exception to the active-search requirement for someone on temporary layoff who expects to be recalled. If that person meets the other conditions, they may count as unemployed without searching for work during the four-week period.","claim":"A temporarily laid-off person expecting recall may qualify without active job search during the four-week period, provided the other conditions are met.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"...made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"The first may qualify without an active search; passive browsing alone does not meet the active-search requirement.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source gives temporary layoff with expected recall as an alternative to active search; the piece retains the other eligibility conditions.","materiality":"Describes a material exception to the active-search criterion."},{"excerpt":"By contrast, someone whose only job-search activity was browsing job listings has not met the active-search requirement.","claim":"Browsing job listings as the sole job-search activity does not satisfy the active-search requirement.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Passive-only job search does not count as active search.","narrative":"...someone whose only job-search activity was browsing listings. The first may qualify without an active search; passive browsing alone does not meet the active-search requirement.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"Browsing listings alone is passive-only search, which the source says does not count as active search.","materiality":"Clarifies which search activity does not satisfy the criterion."},{"excerpt":"Availability and the specified survey periods matter too.","claim":"Availability and the specified survey periods are relevant to unemployment classification.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"...was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week...","narrative":"Clarify that availability and the survey reference periods also matter...","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"Both availability and the reference periods are explicit conditions in the source.","materiality":"Reinforces eligibility criteria."},{"excerpt":"These conditions determine whether someone is counted as unemployed—and included, along with employed people, in the rate’s denominator.","claim":"The stated conditions determine whether someone is counted as unemployed and included with employed people in the rate denominator.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"...that the classification determines whether a person is counted among the unemployed in the rate’s denominator.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The sentence links classification to inclusion in the denominator, consistent with the source’s definition of that denominator.","materiality":"Connects the classification rules to the rate calculation."}]}

